  'Architectural History II surveys modern and contemporary architecture from Enlightenment neoclassicism and industrialization through modernism, postwar reconstruction, postmodernism, late twentieth-century theory, global modernisms, and contemporary debates. Students learn to interpret buildings as formal works, technical systems, social instruments, cultural texts, political artifacts, and historical evidence.',
  $ahi2json${"slug": "architectural-history-ii", "title": "Architectural History II", "description": "Architectural History II surveys modern and contemporary architecture from Enlightenment neoclassicism and industrialization through modernism, postwar reconstruction, postmodernism, late twentieth-century theory, global modernisms, and contemporary debates. Students learn to interpret buildings as formal works, technical systems, social instruments, cultural texts, political artifacts, and historical evidence.", "topics": [{"id": "65ed89d0-d8ef-499e-a23f-1e3612b60437", "type": "topic", "title": "Enlightenment, Neoclassicism, and Revolutionary Space", "description": "This section establishes the transition from pre-modern traditions to modern architectural history. Students learn how archaeology, political revolution, and Enlightenment thought changed the meanings of public buildings, monuments, and cities.", "children": [{"id": "fac2db49-d784-4436-b3a8-e70d2cab5561", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Classical Revival and Archaeological Knowledge", "children": [{"id": "7e0c723b-70b7-47b6-aab9-58306d87a7db", "type": "concept", "title": "Pompeii, Herculaneum, Greece, and archaeological empiricism"}, {"id": "f226f61d-c62a-4fa5-ae32-edd903a3d32f", "type": "concept", "title": "Orders, typology, proportion, and historical accuracy"}, {"id": "b40b28d0-c152-4a89-b5b0-e13c29985ab2", "type": "concept", "title": "Winckelmann, taste, and the authority of antiquity"}, {"id": "2871023e-1998-444d-8825-b5cd98e83501", "type": "concept", "title": "Museums, academies, and architectural education"}], "description": "Neoclassicism used antiquity as evidence, language, and ideology rather than mere decoration."}, {"id": "785c7c90-234c-4410-98fb-04e5318cb5b3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Revolution, Nation, and Public Institutions", "children": [{"id": "de58a738-2546-4bc0-8623-1e92f82904a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Boulee, Ledoux, and visionary architecture"}, {"id": "2567d5ff-e2ce-4f00-94bc-2f66a3f045bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Capitols, museums, libraries, prisons, and hospitals"}, {"id": "e8c47c91-aee3-489f-a375-a2ddfba57186", "type": "concept", "title": "Monumentality, citizenship, and the public sphere"}, {"id": "3be9cfb4-8364-45d6-829c-91a172901f5b", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial and imperial uses of classical language"}], "description": "Architecture became a civic tool for expressing sovereignty, reform, law, and collective memory."}, {"id": "ba919be3-f38a-4492-989a-9acffb237d44", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Neoclassicism's Afterlives", "children": [{"id": "807f3c2e-47cb-4c22-bb38-04aa879f0633", "type": "concept", "title": "Beaux-Arts planning and civic ensembles"}, {"id": "2f7dabc8-d476-4659-a6ad-3cf5021d857b", "type": "concept", "title": "National styles and state power"}, {"id": "ab1ede61-ab81-4314-b171-c95d33ed323f", "type": "concept", "title": "Classicism versus historicism"}, {"id": "b85cf29b-0a2a-46c1-a65d-bc30ffe1d049", "type": "concept", "title": "Neoclassical echoes in modern and postmodern design"}], "description": "Students trace how classical language persisted, mutated, and was challenged in modern architecture."}]}, {"id": "5131c044-f4a9-4669-94c2-eccd07f318f3", "type": "topic", "title": "Industrialization, Iron, Glass, and Infrastructure", "description": "This unit shows that modern architecture emerged from new economies, technologies, and urban conditions. The point is to connect form to production, transportation, materials, labor, and the industrial city.", "children": [{"id": "63765bdc-9588-4351-9ad1-d1b559e3bfc7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "New Materials and Building Systems", "children": [{"id": "66ef948e-9987-4b1e-8c90-910426a7995e", "type": "concept", "title": "Crystal Palace and exhibition architecture"}, {"id": "ee4c2dbf-0552-4692-86d4-2cb014c809ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Railway sheds, factories, warehouses, and markets"}, {"id": "6ff45eb7-e95a-4f1e-8a8b-0159e56aa47b", "type": "concept", "title": "Elevators, fireproofing, and the skyscraper frame"}, {"id": "292eadc3-e9a1-4aa7-8d37-7a4d740b09a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Engineering versus architecture as a modern debate"}], "description": "Iron, steel, glass, elevators, and reinforced concrete opened new spans, heights, programs, and forms of enclosure."}, {"id": "8dbf5837-21af-4f3a-9d27-20ef9ba52967", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Industrial City", "children": [{"id": "10d71bc7-b8da-461f-b11c-ef04699f352a", "type": "concept", "title": "Railroads, ports, bridges, sewers, and utility networks"}, {"id": "a6935eba-9950-443c-a8cb-62ed8f596066", "type": "concept", "title": "Haussmannization and urban modernization"}, {"id": "af4c57c7-cd67-4ef1-975f-bcfd87a65a9a", "type": "concept", "title": "Working-class housing and public health reform"}, {"id": "ae2ca8ff-2210-4744-a8b8-a114bc90461c", "type": "concept", "title": "Planning, zoning, and social control"}], "description": "Students examine how urban growth created problems of sanitation, mobility, housing, labor, and governance."}, {"id": "fc93d545-f788-4f41-98f7-b16454e00deb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Architecture and Capital", "children": [{"id": "ae5854a1-7c87-46f9-ada6-afd2695a5b6b", "type": "concept", "title": "Department stores, arcades, offices, and banks"}, {"id": "64b9d411-868c-4907-8fef-4bccce4ff4bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Expositions and colonial display"}, {"id": "eda70cea-48dc-4e7c-bb97-1698076d3837", "type": "concept", "title": "Speculation, land value, and real estate development"}, {"id": "506d9ddc-c1c7-44a8-a728-a0f62f62d827", "type": "concept", "title": "Labor, construction trades, and industrial production"}], "description": "This subtopic links new building types to capitalism, consumption, bureaucracy, and empire."}]}, {"id": "fed87636-1905-4304-9cc5-52c3275b9091", "type": "topic", "title": "Arts and Crafts, Reform, and Domestic Modernity", "description": "Arts and Crafts offers an early critique of industrial modernity. Students study craft not as nostalgia alone, but as a social, ethical, aesthetic, and political position.", "children": [{"id": "464ae7b3-2c2e-4500-8757-f6ccd9fe3165", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Craft, Labor, and Anti-Industrial Critique", "children": [{"id": "a87893e0-f212-406e-841a-bfdee6be09d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Ruskin, Morris, and the critique of mechanized production"}, {"id": "7be4acee-4eb9-42e4-a504-a8b23605aeae", "type": "concept", "title": "Truth to materials and visible making"}, {"id": "de46cae1-a517-49e5-b0e3-c1af81035fa0", "type": "concept", "title": "Guilds, workshops, and design reform"}, {"id": "8add0d6a-2413-4d3e-86c5-dce92f2bc8d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Contradictions of elite handmade objects"}], "description": "Students learn why craft reformers tied beauty to labor, material honesty, and social life."}, {"id": "649cc34f-4dd5-4c1f-8f81-015d12feb11a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Domestic Architecture and Everyday Life", "children": [{"id": "639d07f3-0152-4efd-8a8b-8c823b74333a", "type": "concept", "title": "Red House and English domestic reform"}, {"id": "eaaf32d4-0b76-4962-ab66-7ee3ba1f4e03", "type": "concept", "title": "Bungalows, garden suburbs, and middle-class domesticity"}, {"id": "dcd0858c-de77-4c2c-86a8-ca4161c30363", "type": "concept", "title": "Interiors, furniture, and integrated design"}, {"id": "6e85f3f1-5506-4280-a7cc-88afa7c3b63d", "type": "concept", "title": "Gendered labor and household reform"}], "description": "The house became a site where modern ideals of comfort, gender, health, and identity were debated."}, {"id": "882cbff0-4c2c-4c21-b1c5-4749ad0275ab", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global Spread and Local Adaptation", "children": [{"id": "36e794f9-144d-43f1-b7b7-21f852b5ca9e", "type": "concept", "title": "American Craftsman and Prairie connections"}, {"id": "95583cb5-3e5b-4044-b533-ba3d28d4443b", "type": "concept", "title": "National romanticism and vernacular revival"}, {"id": "1c3b4dd9-958c-4237-b451-d6ca78c321c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial networks and design education"}, {"id": "6418057b-3c8a-4e34-8d8e-40b0357081bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Craft as resistance and market style"}], "description": "Students track Arts and Crafts through regional movements, colonial settings, and later modernist appropriations."}]}, {"id": "bf4847be-6676-4c4f-9bfc-6a9536c4fc11", "type": "topic", "title": "Art Nouveau, Secession, and the Total Work of Art", "description": "This section examines the search for a new style around 1900. Students learn how ornament, structure, graphic design, urban life, and modern materials converged.", "children": [{"id": "82f1dcc2-7607-4ef2-a020-5c7509480f12", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Organic Ornament and Structural Expression", "children": [{"id": "c72c0da4-bf23-4764-b83a-47b6ed661a8e", "type": "concept", "title": "Horta, Guimard, Gaudi, and Mackintosh"}, {"id": "9c173eea-b30b-458c-97a6-fe9362e765b2", "type": "concept", "title": "Iron ornament, curved lines, and spatial flow"}, {"id": "beb0187e-5338-4075-a0ad-4936ad46bc64", "type": "concept", "title": "Facade, stair, interior, and object integration"}, {"id": "4eacc690-9f73-46e2-bc9a-3cef5acadb81", "type": "concept", "title": "Nature, biology, and abstraction"}], "description": "Art Nouveau tested whether modern architecture could develop forms from nature, structure, and movement."}, {"id": "0e9eb1de-2326-483d-899b-b2d8be621a4e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Secession, Vienna, and Modern Design Culture", "children": [{"id": "7be6e213-f2aa-414c-ab49-5c0bd755f15e", "type": "concept", "title": "Wagner, Hoffmann, Olbrich, and Klimt's milieu"}, {"id": "3e3eb9ad-7a34-4f20-b8a2-114c64623c32", "type": "concept", "title": "Gesamtkunstwerk and total design"}, {"id": "21df3d8b-9bf8-4294-aac8-af6fd1c8c707", "type": "concept", "title": "Ornament, surface, and abstraction"}, {"id": "be238405-04da-440d-ab03-2c2d9c521287", "type": "concept", "title": "Design reform, exhibitions, and journals"}], "description": "Students study how Secession movements turned architecture into a platform for modern art, design, and urban identity."}, {"id": "707b8260-0002-4320-9ab5-621f38a6232f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ornament Debates", "children": [{"id": "d4878fd4-b715-4a71-95ef-c68d804b30c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Loos and Ornament and Crime"}, {"id": "1841186e-9d03-47c8-a40a-ade0fb4cbd85", "type": "concept", "title": "Ornament as labor, crime, identity, and pleasure"}, {"id": "1775810e-fcd1-4e0e-98a8-d29f58093ec4", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender, consumption, and fashion"}, {"id": "541caa7f-c811-4111-9f70-d9d3c928c356", "type": "concept", "title": "From Art Nouveau to modernist reduction"}], "description": "This subtopic sets up later modernism by analyzing the moral and cultural arguments around ornament."}]}, {"id": "67398910-97ef-488f-9b20-8ef037f6c4f8", "type": "topic", "title": "Early Modernism, Abstraction, and Avant-Garde Movements", "description": "This unit introduces the manifestoes, movements, and experiments that turned architecture toward abstraction, technology, and social transformation.", "children": [{"id": "97514d1b-5594-46e3-a647-7b938cdb698b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Avant-Garde Manifestoes and New Forms", "children": [{"id": "2e2ab57a-705c-4d38-8ca5-daae693fd4f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Futurism, Constructivism, De Stijl, and Expressionism"}, {"id": "8d2ea6f1-3f9f-4462-b450-1ce35f51f5e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Manifestoes, diagrams, and exhibitions"}, {"id": "3b27dc1c-582a-4823-bd3a-5d1b8b92b29d", "type": "concept", "title": "Machine aesthetics and speed"}, {"id": "40786872-aca1-42d9-990e-24657cbd0bdc", "type": "concept", "title": "Revolution, utopia, and propaganda"}], "description": "Students learn how architects used polemical writing to imagine new societies and new spatial orders."}, {"id": "8f4870cd-3c7b-4d18-bace-b1ca489580ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Abstraction, Space, and Composition", "children": [{"id": "17123617-c5b3-441f-8dd9-b1ed300f1c99", "type": "concept", "title": "Planar composition and free space"}, {"id": "22d7296a-0a8b-4789-bd09-f20c02c20d00", "type": "concept", "title": "Color, surface, and the dissolution of the box"}, {"id": "3bc5c4ef-d600-41e4-9851-fc30f9e743b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Photography, montage, and visual culture"}, {"id": "33155945-2d17-4317-b76a-5dc51756cc07", "type": "concept", "title": "Painting, sculpture, and architecture exchange"}], "description": "Modern architecture used abstraction to rethink wall, volume, plane, grid, and movement."}, {"id": "4d26709b-fc20-47a3-a5dc-8815f4dd9b54", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Social Programs and the New Human", "children": [{"id": "a6e2a146-075f-4b4c-9ff1-8a9871f028f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Workers' clubs, housing, schools, and sanatoria"}, {"id": "bf11ece4-40f0-4c01-901e-2e1f704b25d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Hygiene, light, air, and health"}, {"id": "2a2ab343-5b98-462b-a42a-0f032b8d5327", "type": "concept", "title": "Standardization and mass production"}, {"id": "9cf73bc0-2e4a-4f02-8120-3806a2eebe0f", "type": "concept", "title": "Utopian promise and authoritarian risk"}], "description": "Students examine the claim that architecture could help remake daily life and collective identity."}]}, {"id": "7b8330b2-2e15-421b-b97e-eb767435050c", "type": "topic", "title": "Le Corbusier, Mies, Wright, and the Canon of High Modernism", "description": "The canonical modernists remain central because their projects shaped architectural education, criticism, and practice. This section teaches the canon while also asking how it became canonical.", "children": [{"id": "d8913d4a-6c12-4cbc-b36e-d6be08d7f22c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Le Corbusier and Purist Modernism", "children": [{"id": "2bd6aafc-6f8a-4504-af40-c5370d533faa", "type": "concept", "title": "Five points, Dom-Ino, and promenade architecturale"}, {"id": "56ffa8e6-c597-43ab-8327-0ed5a58a7333", "type": "concept", "title": "Villa Savoye, Unite d'Habitation, and Chandigarh"}, {"id": "af5d0184-72b6-4a07-8f0f-fbd851207943", "type": "concept", "title": "Plan Voisin and urban tabula rasa"}, {"id": "03d025d1-d932-4b5d-9b20-9a9f7296b15d", "type": "concept", "title": "Purism, colonialism, and political ambiguity"}], "description": "Students study Le Corbusier's formal language, urbanism, media strategy, and changing political contexts."}, {"id": "1c1216c9-1f1c-4b02-b2c9-8e70ecd69518", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mies, Wright, and Alternative Modernisms", "children": [{"id": "6587a9b3-b5c3-4122-a334-4cc69271939e", "type": "concept", "title": "Mies: frame, glass, detail, and universal space"}, {"id": "83d30298-591f-46d1-80f9-648678b5016c", "type": "concept", "title": "Wright: Prairie, Usonian, organic, and Broadacre"}, {"id": "8140b672-dd0e-41e3-9069-8695163b9698", "type": "concept", "title": "House, tower, campus, and pavilion types"}, {"id": "1229fcc3-19ac-426f-8f92-9c6f8736e8e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Material discipline and spatial ideology"}], "description": "This subtopic contrasts universal space, organic architecture, structure, material, and landscape."}, {"id": "9a9e0da6-2546-4ce6-aafd-d63e5e8e2e2e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Canon Formation and Critique", "children": [{"id": "db546aa6-7c02-4a9b-96c0-a84319ae6912", "type": "concept", "title": "Photography, exhibitions, textbooks, and museum narratives"}, {"id": "213498b1-69bd-4a9e-a5de-cb26679cba52", "type": "concept", "title": "Patronage, gender, race, and authorship"}, {"id": "625556f7-44e4-494b-9074-c3445124979c", "type": "concept", "title": "Reception, imitation, and critique"}, {"id": "af307ac3-2f64-4a15-940e-e642d48febee", "type": "concept", "title": "What gets excluded from the modern canon"}], "description": "Students learn to read canonical works critically rather than as untouchable masterpieces."}]}, {"id": "3e38a3c8-6e31-4b55-906f-e86d1c2c4eb3", "type": "topic", "title": "Bauhaus, Design Pedagogy, and Standardization", "description": "This section studies the Bauhaus as school, myth, network, pedagogy, and cultural export. Students learn how design education connected art, craft, industry, politics, and everyday objects.", "children": [{"id": "efee9520-99c3-4b35-aae0-cbd1723cdf41", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Bauhaus Curriculum and Workshops", "children": [{"id": "a0301624-bb82-4d91-812a-c4218e3f3e63", "type": "concept", "title": "Gropius, Meyer, Mies, Itten, Moholy-Nagy, Albers, and Brandt"}, {"id": "972d5c4d-5b79-4598-b9f3-0623968d0994", "type": "concept", "title": "Vorkurs, workshops, and material exercises"}, {"id": "16d07442-b003-4110-9e14-ceec8d4b5fd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Typography, furniture, textiles, theater, and photography"}, {"id": "ac97d435-be40-491f-b639-341b875e5837", "type": "concept", "title": "Collective work and disciplinary integration"}], "description": "Students examine how pedagogy shaped modern design thinking from preliminary courses to workshops."}, {"id": "2c2982a2-4ec9-4b9e-93eb-2458cc293dc4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Standardization, Industry, and Housing", "children": [{"id": "fe154c24-e0d7-4b25-aba1-eb6177a69ea4", "type": "concept", "title": "Dessau building and functional image-making"}, {"id": "98d0e9a8-087b-4aad-94e1-d846505e002f", "type": "concept", "title": "Hannes Meyer and social functionalism"}, {"id": "9635ec64-1855-4806-8585-e176c14308c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Prefabrication, modularity, and industrial design"}, {"id": "77fc49c2-c463-4454-afa4-a23e3b76f09a", "type": "concept", "title": "Housing estates and minimum dwelling debates"}], "description": "The Bauhaus linked design reform to mass production, affordability, and social need."}, {"id": "d66ed990-b582-4a7c-9e11-ca18754b0d2e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Migration, Myth, and Legacy", "children": [{"id": "d3f5fb52-5299-420e-892c-960a7429337b", "type": "concept", "title": "Nazi closure and diaspora"}, {"id": "83efc762-3299-458a-86e7-58509fa22adc", "type": "concept", "title": "New Bauhaus and American design education"}, {"id": "137ee72e-2f22-4632-9257-25517eb2e0a1", "type": "concept", "title": "Corporate modernism and design systems"}, {"id": "6aad2885-134d-40e6-b935-294720b6ff6e", "type": "concept", "title": "Bauhaus as brand, archive, and contested legacy"}], "description": "Students track how Bauhaus figures and ideas moved through exile, schools, corporations, and museums."}]}, {"id": "9b142148-313a-4ad5-8c3e-7b31455db469", "type": "topic", "title": "International Style, CIAM, and Modernism as Global Language", "description": "This unit studies modernism's institutionalization and global circulation. Students learn how a style promoted as universal was shaped by exhibitions, professional networks, colonial conditions, and local adaptation.", "children": [{"id": "022fe1e8-9125-4056-80a8-44cdd4c52982", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Exhibitions, Publications, and the International Style", "children": [{"id": "1746c7a1-4273-4401-8c62-426e2b0a0bae", "type": "concept", "title": "Hitchcock and Johnson's 1932 MoMA exhibition"}, {"id": "e9500b6e-947d-4388-9573-33ae922db2a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Volume, regularity, and anti-ornament"}, {"id": "948114b0-f54a-4656-859e-cf3b6d56e672", "type": "concept", "title": "Photography and white-wall aesthetics"}, {"id": "f13c9dbf-dbcb-4a31-942f-40e619481864", "type": "concept", "title": "Style versus social program"}], "description": "Students analyze how museums, critics, and photographs turned diverse modernisms into a coherent style."}, {"id": "afc172c7-a3cb-4136-bbe0-13405f9a83ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "CIAM, Functional City, and Planning", "children": [{"id": "8428abba-8a33-44ce-b141-e91aa151b764", "type": "concept", "title": "Athens Charter and functional zoning"}, {"id": "ad51fb6e-7d58-4629-b68b-e08f69fd5552", "type": "concept", "title": "Team 10 critique and human association"}, {"id": "6f6d21be-8ad9-4723-a146-9902b0f8e80d", "type": "concept", "title": "Highways, superblocks, and urban renewal"}, {"id": "eafe71fe-ec9e-4c5b-a413-5de633f2523f", "type": "concept", "title": "Planning as expertise and power"}], "description": "This subtopic covers modernist planning as an attempt to rationalize housing, work, recreation, and circulation."}, {"id": "d01cc13d-2ffe-49e1-9798-b20fba1528ee", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modernism in Translation", "children": [{"id": "5fe5c015-8470-412b-9315-75d31f14a496", "type": "concept", "title": "Tropical modernism and brise-soleil"}, {"id": "f50a9c19-9153-4789-9dc6-4ea72c808d83", "type": "concept", "title": "Latin American, African, South Asian, and East Asian modernisms"}, {"id": "1b788634-2cb7-4a07-840b-8295c02b2c46", "type": "concept", "title": "State modernization and nation-building"}, {"id": "21c63730-a610-4cba-94ed-81816964f1ed", "type": "concept", "title": "Local materials, labor, and hybrid forms"}], "description": "Students study how international modernism changed when it encountered climate, craft, politics, economy, and culture."}]}, {"id": "3faba8ff-7975-4617-a8f8-9700777d0e32", "type": "topic", "title": "Housing, Social Reform, and the Modern City", "description": "Housing is a central moral and political test of modern architecture. This section connects design to public health, dignity, density, infrastructure, family life, and the state.", "children": [{"id": "8a4f8ee0-c27d-4ca1-86b4-60812db1eff6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Minimum Dwelling and Social Housing", "children": [{"id": "060be27d-48f9-4e2d-b5e0-548ee98f3906", "type": "concept", "title": "Existenzminimum and Frankfurt kitchen"}, {"id": "eb48ffc1-eea5-41ad-8f5e-0afa390cbfec", "type": "concept", "title": "Siedlungen, slab blocks, and courtyard housing"}, {"id": "cd46a155-0ea7-4e81-a03b-c318c6105495", "type": "concept", "title": "Health, light, air, privacy, and efficiency"}, {"id": "4ce3c74d-4c90-4a6c-86bd-42c8728959c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Tenant life and the limits of expert planning"}], "description": "Students examine how architects defined basic needs and translated them into plans, sections, and policies."}, {"id": "3d274b3c-18b0-4c86-a08f-bc460add105e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Urban Reform and Everyday Infrastructure", "children": [{"id": "9b6ef65a-e0ad-418b-be04-29f07ff0b807", "type": "concept", "title": "Garden city and neighborhood unit"}, {"id": "520e3efb-3f60-4cf4-9fc7-0f5e7cc06dd8", "type": "concept", "title": "Public health and sanitation"}, {"id": "d2a97fd5-3e20-4441-93dc-c99b0f305455", "type": "concept", "title": "Transit-oriented growth and mobility"}, {"id": "2bbe9fa9-8ef4-4ef8-8721-5af1fdc023c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Everyday urbanism and informal life"}], "description": "This subtopic places architecture within streets, transit, schools, parks, markets, and services."}, {"id": "d4cf4cb4-7826-4f1c-84eb-3e7c8fd5b4d8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critiques of Modernist Urbanism", "children": [{"id": "9c4724a2-0d95-48aa-976e-3a58147c30ef", "type": "concept", "title": "Jane Jacobs and sidewalk life"}, {"id": "5bad4679-ec2b-4daa-a245-d29cecdac6fd", "type": "concept", "title": "Pruitt-Igoe and myths of failure"}, {"id": "2f0037f0-0592-4135-b912-8070f09b8592", "type": "concept", "title": "Displacement, segregation, and urban renewal"}, {"id": "0c6c1410-5611-412e-9277-69003348b2f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Participatory design and community control"}], "description": "Students learn why modernist housing and planning drew criticism from residents, activists, and theorists."}]}, {"id": "094781e6-5ba0-4bf0-8633-3b1f6457cd2d", "type": "topic", "title": "Postwar Reconstruction, Brutalism, Megastructure, and Welfare State Architecture", "description": "Postwar architecture responded to ruin, population growth, welfare states, universities, civic programs, and new construction systems. Students learn why concrete, infrastructure, and large-scale form carried both social promise and controversy.", "children": [{"id": "80d0083d-6913-48b4-ba05-adf76aabf804", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reconstruction and Civic Institutions", "children": [{"id": "1df6ed93-8712-4883-a430-d66e51e5bbbf", "type": "concept", "title": "Reconstruction in Europe and Japan"}, {"id": "6651d4f2-ac0c-4746-872a-beaebc2d0720", "type": "concept", "title": "Civic centers, universities, libraries, and cultural buildings"}, {"id": "ec4355d5-a14f-4458-9a2f-479eccf0cc1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Planning authorities and public commissions"}, {"id": "c7966320-c556-4ca9-b442-e230d40eab05", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory, trauma, and rebuilding"}], "description": "Students study architecture as a tool for rebuilding cities, states, and public trust after war."}, {"id": "4d2e43d7-73ed-40ff-9b03-6d26f0c9f653", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Brutalism and Material Ethics", "children": [{"id": "eea75364-155e-4253-bc5f-5a9b5b1db7b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Beton brut, tectonics, and rough materiality"}, {"id": "e39621a5-f361-49a1-9329-e75320886fa3", "type": "concept", "title": "Smithsons, Stirling, Kahn, Rudolph, and others"}, {"id": "59e30597-e637-4e15-8b3c-c4c973b29fcc", "type": "concept", "title": "Universities, housing, and public buildings"}, {"id": "43044af3-c443-4029-aec5-25ddb79c1b07", "type": "concept", "title": "Preservation, stigma, and renewed appreciation"}], "description": "This subtopic treats Brutalism as more than exposed concrete: an ethic of structure, publicness, and institutional form."}, {"id": "9d22fc71-0986-4c55-b870-eaa24fa15412", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Megastructure and Systems Thinking", "children": [{"id": "4b69f7e2-0180-4f04-a383-97f52da12755", "type": "concept", "title": "Archigram, Metabolism, and plug-in visions"}, {"id": "7383fcdc-e751-4595-bab6-ae234c46eb1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Mat-building and flexible systems"}, {"id": "fe4c12fe-39f2-4a81-ae07-6f34761c5397", "type": "concept", "title": "Infrastructure as urban form"}, {"id": "9b484a13-6a35-43c7-a3e9-a7163f2cc66b", "type": "concept", "title": "Failure, maintenance, and social complexity"}], "description": "Students examine architecture at the scale of infrastructure, metabolism, movement, and open-ended growth."}]}, {"id": "57645330-9adf-4ec9-8196-5a9cdee2040a", "type": "topic", "title": "Corporate Modernism, Glass Towers, and Late Capitalist Urbanism", "description": "This section studies the postwar office tower and corporate campus as architectural expressions of management, finance, bureaucracy, globalization, and image.", "children": [{"id": "12d05b1e-51fe-4842-8f80-2e054ad6fd58", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Curtain Wall and the Corporate Tower", "children": [{"id": "52404dc7-b8dc-4b68-9231-0761e343732b", "type": "concept", "title": "Lever House, Seagram Building, and SOM office towers"}, {"id": "0e85e454-476c-43cc-8f3f-9d82480c9e3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Curtain wall technology and repetition"}, {"id": "461b15ed-518f-432f-92e8-e17f20f06e73", "type": "concept", "title": "Plaza bonus, zoning, and public-private space"}, {"id": "90e36ca1-d045-4085-883c-e80a027d77da", "type": "concept", "title": "Corporate identity and architectural restraint"}], "description": "Students examine how structure, facade, lobby, plaza, and skyline produced a new image of power."}, {"id": "caa702b8-224f-4a50-b405-0c7fff1a2b45", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Suburbs, Campuses, and Decentralization", "children": [{"id": "667e1d69-a992-48c0-8576-177308c2de16", "type": "concept", "title": "Office parks and research campuses"}, {"id": "8d398a01-f286-4d22-af39-cbef1597345f", "type": "concept", "title": "Shopping malls and commercial landscapes"}, {"id": "ea53dbec-22e3-4f70-b0be-5a35ce4442a0", "type": "concept", "title": "Highways, parking, and suburbanization"}, {"id": "ecab8f4b-ffc2-4bf5-a819-9bd0eb96a897", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, class, and uneven metropolitan development"}], "description": "This subtopic links postwar architecture to automobiles, corporate research, consumer culture, and metropolitan expansion."}, {"id": "9abe6643-d760-496e-849d-035c5e09d793", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critiques of Late Modern Urbanism", "children": [{"id": "30315c6c-3468-4df8-a4fd-e96b26b1334e", "type": "concept", "title": "Team 10 and human association"}, {"id": "ac06e101-3848-4b01-90b6-458e4ba8b9f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Contextualism and urban morphology"}, {"id": "7a75be1e-5f3d-4fb1-99cb-cf26a47cf1c4", "type": "concept", "title": "Public space versus privatized space"}, {"id": "4929633f-d974-4671-a533-d1d79b21deb6", "type": "concept", "title": "Real estate, spectacle, and governance"}], "description": "Students analyze why efficient modern space was criticized as anonymous, exclusionary, or placeless."}]}, {"id": "552368ce-6f00-4ae4-9999-f64482a749f4", "type": "topic", "title": "Postmodernism, Complexity, Symbol, and Historical Reference", "description": "Postmodern architecture challenged modernist purity through ambiguity, symbolism, history, popular culture, and urban context. Students learn the difference between postmodern style and postmodern critique.", "children": [{"id": "a05ebcc3-0a80-4c5a-8782-99c5473d0c27", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Venturi, Scott Brown, and Complexity", "children": [{"id": "6a87855c-1502-4ada-aea3-2dad29d7ab1d", "type": "concept", "title": "Complexity and Contradiction in Architecture"}, {"id": "3a06bfcc-4399-4c69-b0bb-b7aafe25488e", "type": "concept", "title": "Learning from Las Vegas"}, {"id": "e57f1a08-2e02-4178-9c6c-e92800181cd1", "type": "concept", "title": "Ducks, decorated sheds, signs, and symbolism"}, {"id": "3c45ae9e-adcc-494a-bbc8-4d7a35daf8b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Denise Scott Brown, authorship, and recognition"}], "description": "Students study the argument that architecture should embrace contradiction, ordinary landscapes, signs, and multiple meanings."}, {"id": "5b213bed-f53c-4f4f-858e-bebb80caf802", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical Reference and Urban Context", "children": [{"id": "aedb9018-e97f-4885-b6c5-c760320da954", "type": "concept", "title": "Rossi, Krier, Moore, Graves, Stirling, and Bofill"}, {"id": "e390ec07-2f80-4300-a266-e9f7b5bd3bed", "type": "concept", "title": "Typology, memory, and the city"}, {"id": "f6097b73-8f68-4c3c-b916-695569b3c482", "type": "concept", "title": "Facade, ornament, color, and quotation"}, {"id": "599b84cd-e3bb-4497-afc8-91d0b06f11a0", "type": "concept", "title": "Pastiche, irony, and populism"}], "description": "This subtopic examines postmodern returns to ornament, type, street, monument, and memory."}, {"id": "4d786eca-06e2-4fbb-bab1-f60d502765e3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Postmodernism's Political Ambiguity", "children": [{"id": "78dfb5e3-b15e-4ec8-822d-cb0783ae8202", "type": "concept", "title": "Critique of universal modernism"}, {"id": "a122e254-9dac-4e9a-a7f8-009289804d24", "type": "concept", "title": "Market imagery and themed environments"}, {"id": "172e07be-32d3-4309-8899-03ff205990e5", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity, pluralism, and representation"}, {"id": "649cda3d-c3f9-4116-b2b4-2ffa77c9bbc7", "type": "concept", "title": "Postmodern preservation and heritage culture"}], "description": "Students evaluate postmodernism's liberating critiques and its links to consumer spectacle."}]}, {"id": "20f4d84c-ab08-4204-863a-cebf34e45d5f", "type": "topic", "title": "Deconstructivism, Critical Theory, and Late 20th-Century Discourse", "description": "This unit connects architecture to language, philosophy, media, institutional critique, and the late twentieth-century theory boom. Students learn to read buildings and texts together.", "children": [{"id": "a23b4366-fd04-4f45-b6d2-9a364e695110", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Deconstructivist Form and Theory", "children": [{"id": "15c7f99b-f170-45e6-bd65-777e548beadd", "type": "concept", "title": "Derrida, Eisenman, Tschumi, Libeskind, Hadid, Coop Himmelb(l)au"}, {"id": "4ca568f3-fb38-4516-9d15-d1da15b9fb81", "type": "concept", "title": "MoMA 1988 Deconstructivist Architecture exhibition"}, {"id": "31109198-4b6c-4ddb-8944-3dc5286db4fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Grid, trace, event, and disjunction"}, {"id": "109a1c3a-fc24-4db4-8e56-7b611c0b2a22", "type": "concept", "title": "Form, philosophy, and spectacle"}], "description": "Students study fragmentation, instability, and dislocation as formal and theoretical strategies."}, {"id": "5ec7e644-c6d6-4588-a243-577b7ff5a919", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critical Theory and Architectural Texts", "children": [{"id": "b7fe1bf0-68d0-4e23-8912-e87fb4e4ce7a", "type": "concept", "title": "Semiotics, phenomenology, Marxism, feminism, and poststructuralism"}, {"id": "a9ef6230-3412-47f8-b59c-6c8a43221f75", "type": "concept", "title": "Architecture Theory Since 1968"}, {"id": "711d83ac-7bb3-4c40-8698-27b53346589a", "type": "concept", "title": "Close reading and concept mapping"}, {"id": "aea4df65-a863-43ec-bb56-406ee2ccfd23", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory's relation to studio culture"}], "description": "This subtopic trains students to read theory as evidence and argument rather than as jargon."}, {"id": "3e41c623-6b81-4930-b7f8-60c3bfceab80", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Late 20th-Century Institutions and Media", "children": [{"id": "15986af7-4881-4c07-a36f-a1b1b8968ee5", "type": "concept", "title": "Oppositions, Assemblage, Log, and other venues"}, {"id": "fe747314-8b1d-4224-9a90-c9c9aca023df", "type": "concept", "title": "Curators, critics, and academic networks"}, {"id": "1d76f4a2-86e7-43ee-b14a-add8a505901c", "type": "concept", "title": "Drawings, models, and publication culture"}, {"id": "7455993e-8b95-4cad-b4a8-ed39fd30ee5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Star systems and institutional power"}], "description": "Students analyze how journals, exhibitions, schools, biennales, and magazines shaped architectural discourse."}]}, {"id": "9766fef4-c3a2-4a12-a1b1-3e21ce8ba724", "type": "topic", "title": "Global Modernisms, Colonialism, Decolonial Critique, and Critical Regionalism", "description": "This section expands the course beyond a Euro-American sequence. Students learn to study modern architecture as global exchange shaped by empire, migration, climate, materials, nationalism, and resistance.", "children": [{"id": "aeb3807b-240b-49ae-8d4b-eb2448fbd027", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Colonial Modernity and Architectural Exchange", "children": [{"id": "1855d474-5277-4e44-9619-24bea8b22925", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial capitals, schools, hospitals, housing, and infrastructure"}, {"id": "be30508c-a8af-45a5-bf9a-09cb0a950cff", "type": "concept", "title": "Missionary, administrative, and commercial architecture"}, {"id": "c36d3a21-e61f-4ba0-a211-6da11f008e72", "type": "concept", "title": "Tropical expertise and climatic design"}, {"id": "0c60c565-d26b-45ec-95b5-28204cd75000", "type": "concept", "title": "Archives, extraction, and asymmetrical exchange"}], "description": "Students examine how colonial power shaped buildings, planning, labor, and knowledge circulation."}, {"id": "d93ddd8a-10fa-4467-a10e-8d6f6dcf3a53", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Postcolonial Nation-Building and Global Modernisms", "children": [{"id": "45d14344-30e7-4b2b-93f7-95bbc2754ee0", "type": "concept", "title": "Chandigarh, Brasilia, Accra, Dakar, Mexico City, Tokyo, and beyond"}, {"id": "bc3bd174-2515-4ddf-a43e-ca121cef740b", "type": "concept", "title": "State modernity and development planning"}, {"id": "3952c6e2-8cb9-41c2-be8b-1d33bd335c8f", "type": "concept", "title": "Local architects and transnational networks"}, {"id": "84080e3d-c924-4ccc-b9eb-2ea53e1029e6", "type": "concept", "title": "Modernism outside the center-periphery model"}], "description": "This subtopic studies modern architecture as a tool of independence, development, and cultural identity."}, {"id": "03855668-b4f6-4c56-bc9c-98c325536205", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critical Regionalism and Decolonial Critique", "children": [{"id": "fb8ccd8e-c43d-4101-810e-48b252d9a461", "type": "concept", "title": "Frampton's critical regionalism"}, {"id": "56b8616c-1272-45e7-b5d4-35eaf07fae97", "type": "concept", "title": "Place, tectonics, topography, and climate"}, {"id": "8d20d9c6-8a93-4bb0-adaa-42fce89bc6d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Indigenous, vernacular, and local knowledge"}, {"id": "624b2f61-4601-477c-81c6-4812e3345821", "type": "concept", "title": "Risks of essentialism and exoticism"}], "description": "Students assess critical regionalism and decolonial frameworks as methods for reading place, climate, culture, and power."}]}, {"id": "87d43194-bc57-4167-ad77-be858d274f4d", "type": "topic", "title": "Race, Gender, Labor, and Architectural Production", "description": "This unit asks who appears in architectural history and who has been made invisible. Students study architecture as collective production involving designers, clients, builders, occupants, critics, and institutions.", "children": [{"id": "65c09ea3-021f-46fb-bd6f-4d66d1765051", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race, Segregation, and Spatial Inequality", "children": [{"id": "14a07e22-4101-4146-85a2-839129897577", "type": "concept", "title": "Redlining, urban renewal, and highway construction"}, {"id": "1ddd450f-1912-4c27-8765-709938cc562b", "type": "concept", "title": "Public housing and discriminatory policy"}, {"id": "5bf6b2a3-2da0-4633-ad5f-a5abc8f4c4ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Black, Indigenous, and minority architects"}, {"id": "1adae8a8-30c4-4264-9d80-7d34537bab7c", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory, monuments, and spatial justice"}], "description": "Students connect architecture and planning to racial capitalism, segregation, displacement, and public policy."}, {"id": "01fc3e88-e8ce-4978-b326-208b7e003ee6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender, Authorship, and Professional Gatekeeping", "children": [{"id": "f47c4ecc-2aef-4d63-a226-e16ea461c407", "type": "concept", "title": "Women in modern architecture and design"}, {"id": "755f6017-45c7-4d06-a098-d51a43724307", "type": "concept", "title": "Invisible collaborators and office labor"}, {"id": "9a7973bf-e135-4794-aec1-a8e3276b9275", "type": "concept", "title": "Domesticity, care, and feminist space"}, {"id": "c4f175ae-2450-4c08-a52e-52570d2406ae", "type": "concept", "title": "Queer space and critique of normative environments"}], "description": "This subtopic examines how gender shaped access to education, credit, practice, domestic design, and historiography."}, {"id": "be3e5fc6-f464-406a-bda5-a31ca427b655", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Labor, Construction, and Material Supply Chains", "children": [{"id": "8e76f6bf-085e-4c7b-a3e1-d98be4e7aca5", "type": "concept", "title": "Construction workers, contractors, and fabricators"}, {"id": "2be14d45-b66f-4f40-b53d-b4821789d540", "type": "concept", "title": "Material extraction and embodied labor"}, {"id": "141a7050-bacc-45a9-b75c-d1902a7189be", "type": "concept", "title": "Maintenance, cleaning, and operations"}, {"id": "f592b46c-2c3f-409d-8077-060ccbd6f56c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics of authorship and credit"}], "description": "Students learn to see buildings as products of labor, extraction, craft, maintenance, and risk."}]}, {"id": "f3a109b7-193a-474b-b30d-c453a81e6b4e", "type": "topic", "title": "Sustainability, Technology, Media, and Ecological Thought", "description": "This section links modern architectural history to energy, media, systems, environmentalism, and technological imagination. Students learn how environmental questions were present before contemporary sustainability branding.", "children": [{"id": "af038b68-2e28-4033-9030-d2b321f7000f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Environmental Systems and Energy Histories", "children": [{"id": "0f24542c-acfb-461e-9bf8-caa327a951c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Sun, shade, ventilation, and climate-responsive design"}, {"id": "fee24e50-6814-44ed-9008-a9880d3d1fd0", "type": "concept", "title": "Air conditioning and sealed interiors"}, {"id": "3304b5a6-87d5-45fe-b161-71219367c7ec", "type": "concept", "title": "Energy crisis and ecological design"}, {"id": "2ae4dd49-7c23-4584-97a0-28f28acafa5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Carbon, retrofit, and preservation ethics"}], "description": "Students trace passive design, climate control, energy abundance, and oil-era building culture."}, {"id": "b4f2fc07-0568-4655-a6fd-dc675a1312d8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Technology, Media, and Architectural Modernity", "children": [{"id": "b391350a-e551-40cb-b31a-882e46c10bd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Photography and the modern canon"}, {"id": "c3bbe855-2c1e-4dd4-ac9a-ae9cc1a4cbd1", "type": "concept", "title": "Film, television, magazines, and mass culture"}, {"id": "9aaae71c-00f2-44ab-b9ab-75e1be1e9185", "type": "concept", "title": "CAD, parametric design, and digital fabrication"}, {"id": "7863269b-d3b4-427d-8dbf-8b4a81a54a84", "type": "concept", "title": "Media images versus lived buildings"}], "description": "This subtopic studies photography, film, drawing, computation, and digital media as forces that shape architecture's history."}, {"id": "087dadae-3873-4caa-989a-34a0188c05c9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ecological Thought and More-than-Building Scales", "children": [{"id": "f8885d0e-9258-4b61-9b4b-90518a1e3668", "type": "concept", "title": "Landscape urbanism and ecological planning"}, {"id": "7c46e634-f501-46a2-b0af-6cc4f744f875", "type": "concept", "title": "Infrastructure, logistics, and resource flows"}, {"id": "1b2b09f0-a377-4645-a054-f7cf775cf3d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Waste, reuse, and adaptive transformation"}, {"id": "ada06bc1-535c-4db0-a896-0cd7a319c4e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Environmental justice and climate vulnerability"}], "description": "Students consider architecture as part of landscapes, infrastructures, territories, and planetary systems."}]}, {"id": "6bd08527-cbd3-4578-8d36-db849c58739b", "type": "topic", "title": "Contemporary Architecture, Digital Culture, Starchitects, and Public Space", "description": "This unit studies architecture after the late twentieth century through globalization, digital tools, spectacle, inequality, cultural institutions, and renewed debates about public space.", "children": [{"id": "bf30510f-b533-46f1-9196-c5b445b0d208", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital Form and Fabrication", "children": [{"id": "568b966e-024b-4b34-a598-3612328e9cb0", "type": "concept", "title": "Parametricism, scripting, and nonstandard form"}, {"id": "0b7ef28f-3b21-4bb1-96fd-095c35d4a15a", "type": "concept", "title": "BIM, fabrication, and prototyping"}, {"id": "6331b803-0521-4e43-af8a-c81399b28ee1", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital image culture and rendering"}, {"id": "33e47a8b-0483-4aef-a53b-3d78a7c6e341", "type": "concept", "title": "Formal experimentation versus performance claims"}], "description": "Students examine how computation changed design process, form, documentation, and construction."}, {"id": "9777ad84-5d7e-4ba6-90c1-25761fd3f3a5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Starchitects, Icons, and Cultural Economies", "children": [{"id": "e5285366-151f-4c24-89bf-42b3abff17a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Guggenheim Bilbao and the Bilbao effect"}, {"id": "fedd71e3-c96f-489f-9863-be2e1653fd77", "type": "concept", "title": "Museums, airports, campuses, and luxury districts"}, {"id": "d766660f-f13e-4249-b617-79dff250ed0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Global firms and transnational commissions"}, {"id": "d470cad7-c0dd-43e8-affb-23e7b1950540", "type": "concept", "title": "Labor, cost, maintenance, and civic value"}], "description": "This subtopic analyzes iconic architecture as city branding, tourism, cultural capital, and global practice."}, {"id": "673c25d2-3e36-4a2e-9abe-068c5da0145b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public Space, Participation, and Social Practice", "children": [{"id": "875a48bb-dbbb-429d-bcfa-0837dfa8acbe", "type": "concept", "title": "Squares, streets, parks, and civic interiors"}, {"id": "06c9d291-a00a-4336-b9a7-4e5f84495510", "type": "concept", "title": "Temporary urbanism and tactical interventions"}, {"id": "ffcc2cc2-a2b7-4f71-a444-343d213265f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Participatory design and community-based practice"}, {"id": "4cd66546-8ae0-4ce1-8d4f-aa8f81223388", "type": "concept", "title": "Architecture in protest, migration, and crisis"}], "description": "Students study contemporary responses to privatization, protest, informality, and community design."}]}, {"id": "da1c7eba-a027-4fd8-a81c-2ea8da2e2241", "type": "topic", "title": "Historiography, Visual Analysis, and Research Paper Lab", "description": "The final section teaches students how to produce architectural history, not just consume it. The goal is a research argument grounded in buildings, images, texts, archives, and critical methods.", "children": [{"id": "2d716a52-cc7f-4135-b773-04074f84d013", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Visual and Spatial Analysis", "children": [{"id": "9e45f02a-9c0e-420f-943c-f6477f528ab1", "type": "concept", "title": "Formal analysis and spatial sequence"}, {"id": "a69512a9-418b-4642-a6da-005e6e6bed92", "type": "concept", "title": "Plan-section-elevation reading"}, {"id": "86c8a29b-3bbd-404c-b146-17b0d986a1d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Photographic evidence and its limits"}, {"id": "6b0c897b-d6fb-44fd-80be-7e880210fb58", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative precedent analysis"}], "description": "Students learn to read buildings through drawings, photographs, plans, sections, elevations, materials, and site relations."}, {"id": "91f74774-bd39-4bfe-843f-c1eb5cc0ecf1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historiography and Source Critique", "children": [{"id": "f81569f1-f20a-4f42-9510-0528310c679f", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary versus secondary sources"}, {"id": "3bec11e8-59a1-4398-b565-ff19e679c249", "type": "concept", "title": "Archives, oral histories, exhibitions, and collections"}, {"id": "ce2330e3-0ff4-43eb-8c9a-958b5cec215f", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon critique and missing evidence"}, {"id": "9bda100a-62ba-49b2-bf28-86f2da8d3d32", "type": "concept", "title": "Periodization, geography, and method"}], "description": "This subtopic asks how histories are written, archived, canonized, and revised."}, {"id": "d5b917b2-ba37-4f83-9484-ab5ce8a13463", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research Paper and Presentation", "children": [{"id": "0b20ab7c-0987-471b-93fa-ea1aabc38a3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Research question and thesis"}, {"id": "f2640a62-5ccd-48a6-a843-25b0ed3631a3", "type": "concept", "title": "Annotated bibliography and source map"}, {"id": "f9c9a282-e4a5-48c6-8871-56e9f0b25689", "type": "concept", "title": "Close reading of buildings and texts"}, {"id": "df7da048-a77a-484e-a4c1-62351e63bbcb", "type": "concept", "title": "Argument, evidence, citations, and revision"}], "description": "Students synthesize a defensible argument about a building, architect, movement, city, or debate."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$ahi2json$::jsonb,
